<script lang="ts">
  export let onClose:()=>void;
  function open(node:HTMLDialogElement){node.showModal();return{destroy(){node.close()}}}
</script>
<dialog use:open onclose={onClose} onclick={(event)=>{if(event.target===event.currentTarget)onClose();}} aria-label="FAQ">
  <header><h2>FAQ</h2><button aria-label="Close FAQ" onclick={onClose}>×</button></header>
  <div class="faq-body">
    <details><summary>What is Umalytics?</summary><p>It’s a replay and telemetry viewer for Uma Musume Pretty Derby on Steam Global. The Windows app can collect races while you play. This website viewer opens the same race JSON without installing anything. Either way you get the replay, stats, skills, hills, traffic, and the rest of the analysis.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Where do I download it, and where does it save files?</summary><p>The Windows app is <strong>Umalytics.exe</strong> from the GitHub releases page: <a href="https://github.com/exKitsune/Umalytics/releases/latest" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">github.com/exKitsune/Umalytics/releases/latest</a>. The web viewer is <a href="https://exkitsune.github.io/Umalytics/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">exkitsune.github.io/Umalytics</a>.</p><p>On Windows, races, settings, and the capture certificate key live in <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Umalytics</code>. Keep that folder private. Closing the window keeps collection running in the tray; use Exit in the tray menu to stop it.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How does race capture work?</summary><p>Umalytics only listens to Uma Musume game API traffic on your computer. It doesn’t scrape other apps or send your races anywhere. When the game returns a replay, we save that race JSON — the same kind of file you can import, export, or drop onto this window.</p><p>First-time setup is a one-time capture certificate plus a Steam launch option so the game talks through that local listener.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can I use the JSON with Hakuraku?</summary><p>Yes. The exported race JSON is the same portable replay format Hakuraku uses. You can take a race out of Umalytics and open it there, or bring a Hakuraku JSON back here.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How accurate is the analysis?</summary><p>The recorded replay is the source of truth for positions, HP, skills, and the field. A lot of the extra numbers — utilization, inferred modes, time saved, missed-skill reasons, last-spurt chances — are derived from that replay plus game reference data. We do that as carefully as we can, but treat derived results as at most 99.9% accurate. Unknown modes, missing inputs, and overlapping effects are why you’ll still see ranges and “unknown” labels.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What does simulated speed show?</summary><p>The dashed line is her free-running speed if nobody else were in the way. Acceleration, section speed, skills, debuffs, slopes, and supported statuses are in; traffic is out. The shaded band is the spread when a wit roll, pace mode, or downhill mode is still unknown. That white or black line is reserved for this overlay — it isn’t a runner color. Final-spurt targets use the HP budget and the speed plateaus we can support. We don’t average those with cruising speed when a max spurt is on the table.</p><p>Skill gains compare the same model with and without that skill firing. Recorded positions, HP, and the rest of the field stay put. It’s a local comparison, not a predicted different finish.</p></details>
    <details open><summary>What does utilization mean?</summary><p>For target-speed skills: 0% while she’s blocked or still below normal speed, 50% while she’s accelerating from normal speed up to the boosted target, and 100% once she’s there. If she keeps some of that speed while slowing down, that still counts as full use. We compare paths with and without the skill, so leftover speed from something else doesn’t get counted twice.</p><p>Normal speed already includes her other skills and debuffs. This score is not the same as metres gained. Acceleration is useful while she’s still climbing toward the cap; sitting at the cap isn’t using it. Mixed skills show each effect separately.</p><p>For recoveries, HP efficiency is how much HP actually stuck after the cap. It doesn’t try to score the recovery’s whole-race value.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Why does recovery timing matter?</summary><p>A recovery can dump all its HP and still arrive too late — after she’s already delayed or taken a slower last spurt. Late recoveries can change the spurt plan, but the replay doesn’t tell us each internal speed choice.</p><p>The max-spurt HP budget compares HP before and after the recovery against a calculated cost to 60 m before the finish. Uphills are in; acceleration, future skills, and random modes are out. Covering the budget doesn’t prove the game picked that speed.</p><p>Recovery details show timing, wasted HP, and nearby speed changes. Those speed changes can also come from other skills, hills, or blocking — they’re not all from the recovery.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What is speed carryover?</summary><p>Speed effects can leave extra speed that still helps through a later acceleration or slowdown. That leftover distance is carryover. For acceleration skills, the full head start counts as direct gain even after the timer ends.</p><p>Carryover is counted once in the total. The percentage is leftover distance ÷ speed potential (full speed bonus × duration). A 0.5 m/s bonus for 4 seconds has 2 m of potential, so 0.4 m of carryover is 20%. Mixed skills only use their speed portion. Acceleration doesn’t get a carryover percentage.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How is skill duration calculated?</summary><p>Base duration × race distance ÷ 1000, then any special duration modifiers we know about. A 3-second skill lasts 7.5 seconds in a 2500 m race before those extra modifiers. Skill level changes how strong the effect is, not this base duration.</p><p>If the replay has timing, we use that. Recoveries and other instant effects happen once. Their badges linger for a moment so you can see them — that isn’t an active duration.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How does wit affect section speed?</summary><p>The race is split into 24 equal-distance sections. Each one gets a random target-speed tweak based on starting wit. Those tweaks stop once she’s in her selected final spurt.</p><p>Avg. section speed is the expected tweak, not something we measured. The replay doesn’t report the individual rolls. Skills, acceleration, and blocking can also keep her from reaching that target.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Why is a result partial or unavailable?</summary><p>The replay records what happened, not every internal speed target. We also can’t always resolve early position-keeping, blocking transitions, or overlapping effects.</p><p>A range includes unknown time. 83–100% use means 83% was useful and the other 17% we can’t place. The striped part of the bar is that uncertainty — not a broken replay or a failed skill.</p><p>Skill details show why. Unknown time is kept separate from time spent at the speed cap or after the finish.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Are distance and time gains exact?</summary><p>They compare the replay to a calculation that removes one effect and leaves the other known effects alone. Time saved is the travel-time equivalent of the distance gain, not a predicted finish.</p><p>Don’t add overlapping skill and status gains together.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How are hills calculated?</summary><p>Uphill target-speed loss is slope × 200 ÷ power, with slope as a percentage. More power means less loss. The power comparison uses 1000 power.</p><p>Downhill mode adds 0.3 + |slope| ÷ 10 m/s and uses 40% of normal HP drain. Being on a downhill stretch doesn’t mean downhill mode is on.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How are outer-corner losses calculated?</summary><p>We compare her path to lane 0 around each corner. Extra distance comes from the recorded lane and the course’s turning angle. Equivalent time uses her recorded speed.</p><p>That’s 11.25 metres per lane unit, from game constants and the reference mechanics. It doesn’t include weaving or changed race decisions, so it isn’t an exact finish-time loss.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What does the Rushed bonus show?</summary><p>Rushed can bump the target by 4% of base strategy speed. The distance is how much of that extra speed she actually ran. Time spent blocked, or still below the ordinary target, adds nothing. While she’s still climbing onto the 4%, only the surplus she’s already reached counts. If we don’t know the spurt target, the window stays unavailable.</p><p>Extra HP compares the same speed trace with the rushed drain taken out. Missing required inputs leave this unavailable.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Does the replay report downhill and position-keep modes?</summary><p>This replay format reports position, speed, HP, rushed, and front blocking. It doesn’t directly report DH, PU, PD, SU, or overtake.</p><p>DH is inferred from reduced HP drain on a downhill, after recoveries and debuffs. Position-keep modes use skill-adjusted speed, strategy, and gaps. Their distance and HP gains compare paths with and without the mode, including acceleration and traffic. Unknown stretches stay uncertain. Not seeing a mode doesn’t prove it was off.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How are blocking and exhaustion losses calculated?</summary><p>Traffic loss compares recorded speed to a free-running path that starts at the same speed before the slowdown. It includes braking before a recorded block and the time to get back up to speed. Being nearby isn’t enough — the front distance and lane gap have to fit the collision rule.</p><p>Exhaustion compares the recorded run to one that still has HP. Ranges cover unknown speed targets; partial results only cover time we can support. These can overlap, so adding them can count the same lost metres twice.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How do you check HP debuffs?</summary><p>We compare the recorded HP drop to normal running drain, then to running drain plus the debuff. Speed, ground, race phase, and supported drain modifiers all go into that.</p><p>A hit is supported when only the damage calc fits. Immunity is supported when only normal drain fits. If both fit, or we’re missing inputs, it stays unknown. This doesn’t assign teams.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Why didn’t a skill activate?</summary><p>We check its conditions across the race. A red × means we didn’t find an activation. Failed conditions and wit failures are listed separately. An inferred wit failure needs a complete recording and a guaranteed chance to activate under the model.</p><p>Every condition rule in the bundled Global skill data has been reviewed. Some replays still don’t have the team, NPC, or random-trigger data we need. Knowing a rule doesn’t fill those gaps.</p><p>Guaranteed passives count as active when their conditions are met, even if the replay never lists a separate activation. Some starting acceleration skills can also be inferred when the initial marker, conditions, and recorded acceleration all agree. Those are labeled inferred.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What do the replay effects show?</summary><p>Skill bars show time remaining. Colored badges mark active statuses. Debuff badges are for recipients listed in the replay.</p><p>Vision cones are the skill’s range. Someone inside a cone is a possible target; a recorded hit confirms it.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Which game version do the calculations use?</summary><p>Steam Global. Later game updates only get added once they’re supported for this version.</p></details>
  </div>
</dialog>
